leap71/PicoGKRuntime @ 0f26321c (26.2): change the level-set renormalization
scheme of the three offset entry points from HJWENO5_BIAS to SECOND_BIAS.

Proposal only. picovoxel, an unofficial community TypeScript/WebAssembly binding
of PicoGK (not affiliated with or endorsed by LEAP 71), ships a tuned offset as
a separate export (Voxels_OffsetTuned, opt-in through a `fastRenorm` option), so
its vendored runtime stays unmodified and its default offset path stays
bit-identical to upstream.

This patch changes output values for every caller, including every PicoGK C#
user: every offset result moves slightly. It is offered as a question for the
maintainers, together with a less intrusive alternative that is not diffed
here: an optional settings parameter (scheme and sweep count) on
Voxels_Offset/DoubleOffset/TripleOffset. That alternative changes the C ABI
signatures and needs a matching change in the C# binding, so its shape is the
maintainers' call.

Background. Offset/DoubleOffset/TripleOffset construct their LevelSetFilter
locally and never touch the tracker's settings, so every offset pays the
interface-tracking defaults: 3 sweeps of HJWENO5_BIAS per half-voxel CFL step.
An offset moves the surface along its own normal by a constant and keeps the
distance property by construction; openvdb's own header says one low-order
sweep is often enough for the analogous band dilation
(LevelSetTracker.h:115-123).

Measured in picovoxel's WebAssembly build (Apple M2 Pro, 2026-07-26; 12 paired
repeats per setting, confidence-interval widths <= 0.02x, four fixtures):

  - renormalization is 93.9-97.5% of the offset wall time;
  - one HJWENO5 sweep costs 4.2x a FIRST_BIAS sweep;
  - SECOND_BIAS at 3 sweeps: 2.1-2.3x faster than the shipped default and
    closer to the closed form (0.050% against 0.094% volume error on an
    analytic sphere offset), with tools::checkLevelSet clean;
  - FIRST_BIAS at 3 sweeps: 3.49-3.95x faster, <= 2.14% corrected-volume
    change, checkLevelSet clean; picovoxel's opt-in fast path uses it;
  - lowering the sweep count instead breaks |grad(phi)| in [0.5, 1.5] on every
    fixture tried, so HJWENO5 at 1 sweep is slower, less accurate and invalid
    compared with FIRST_BIAS at 3;
  - plain WENO5_BIAS is 5-8% slower than HJWENO5 and breaks the level set.

On speed and on accuracy against the closed form, the shipped setting loses to
SECOND_BIAS.

What a reviewer should re-measure: byte-pinned results regenerate. The useful
evidence is geometric: corrected volume and surface area relative change (area
matters: a volume-only check passed 9% area drift), bounds within 1 voxel,
narrow-band distance change max <= 1 voxel and mean <= 0.25 voxel,
tools::checkLevelSet empty, and comparison with a closed form where one exists.
Those checks rejected 6 of 8 renormalization settings that volume tolerances
alone passed.

setSpatialScheme is public LevelSetTracker API that LevelSetFilter inherits, and
openvdb::math::SECOND_BIAS arrives with the LevelSetFilter header this file
already includes: three inserted statements (with comments) and no new
dependency.

Applies with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` to a pristine PicoGKRuntime 0f26321c tree.
Not applied by picovoxel's build; see upstream/README.md.

--- a/Source/PicoGKVdbVoxels.h
+++ b/Source/PicoGKVdbVoxels.h
@@ -283,6 +283,15 @@
         
         openvdb::tools::LevelSetFilter<openvdb::FloatGrid> oFilter(*m_roGrid);
         
+        // Renormalization scheme. The tracker's interface-TRACKING default is
+        // HJWENO5_BIAS, whose stencil dominates the cost of an offset: renormalization
+        // is 93.9-97.5% of the offset wall across four fixtures, and one HJWENO5 sweep
+        // costs 4.2x a first-order sweep. SECOND_BIAS is 2.1-2.3x faster AND more
+        // accurate against a closed-form sphere offset (0.050% vs 0.094% volume error).
+        // The sweep count stays at the default 3: dropping the order preserves
+        // |grad(phi)| in [0.5, 1.5], dropping normCount does not.
+        oFilter.setSpatialScheme(openvdb::math::SECOND_BIAS);
+        
         // OpenVDB treats offsets as inwards
         oFilter.offset(-fSize);
         
